  "summary": "Centralia (WA) Police Department will continue operating its Flock Safety ALPR cameras despite new Washington state restrictions under SB 6002, which limit ALPR use to felonies and gross misdemeanors, shorten retention to 21 days, and bar cameras within 500 feet of schools, places of worship, reproductive health facilities, and courthouses. Centralia shut off three cameras that violated the proximity rule but will keep the rest; Chief Andy Caldwell still calls the technology a 'force multiplier.' The article contrasts Centralia with Seattle, Kent, and Pierce County agencies that temporarily shut down their systems to comply.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Centralia Police Chief Andy Caldwell still considers the technology a \"force multiplier\" even with the new restrictions.",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "The Centralia Police Department has three cameras that violated that rule that have since been shut off.",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "the local department will no longer get notifications from the National Criminal Information Center (NCIC) when vehicles associated with individuals on the national watchlist enter the area",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    }
